BT
/F1 16 Tf 1 0 0 1 50 800 Tm (Chapter 4 - Simple Harmonic Motion: Formula Sheet) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 50 772 Tm () Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 50 749 Tm (Simple harmonic motion \(SHM\) is a periodic motion in which the acceleration) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 50 726 Tm (is directly proportional to the displacement and acts towards the mean position.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 50 703 Tm (Definition: a = -w^2 x, where w is the angular frequency.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 50 680 Tm (Displacement: x = A sin\(wt + phi\). A is called the amplitude.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 50 657 Tm (Time period of a simple pendulum: T = 2 pi sqrt\(L / g\).) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 50 634 Tm (The time period of a simple pendulum does not depend on the mass of the bob.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 50 611 Tm (Spring-mass system: T = 2 pi sqrt\(m / k\).) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 50 588 Tm (Springs in series: 1/k = 1/k1 + 1/k2. Springs in parallel: k = k1 + k2.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 50 565 Tm (Energy in simple harmonic motion: total energy E = \(1/2\) k A^2 stays constant.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 50 542 Tm (Kinetic energy is maximum at the mean position; potential energy is maximum at the extremes.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 50 519 Tm (Important: in simple harmonic motion the velocity is maximum at the mean position.) Tj
ET
